  <!-- ── The application process ──────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>How Family Members Apply on a Student Visa</h2>
        <p>The process differs from applying alone. Family members typically apply as dependants, meaning they cannot apply independently — their application is tied to the student visa.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__steps">
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">1</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Clarify with your consulate early</h3>
            <p>Before preparing any documents, contact your specific consulate and ask: "Can family members apply as dependants on a student visa?" and "What are the specific requirements?" Not all consulates handle family applications, and some have stricter criteria than others. Getting clarity now saves months of wasted preparation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">2</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Prepare documents for all family members</h3>
            <p>Each family member prepares their own document package (passport, relationship proof, criminal record, health insurance, etc.). All documents need apostille and sworn Spanish translation. Organize documents clearly so the consulate can easily match each dependant to the student application.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">3</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Apply together at the consulate</h3>
            <p>In most cases, the student and all family member dependants apply together at the same consulate appointment. Each person submits their own application form, but the applications are linked. The main student is the principal applicant, and family members are listed as dependants requesting residence based on that principal application.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">4</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Demonstrate financial capacity for the entire household</h3>
            <p>As part of the application, you must prove that the combined household (you + family members) has sufficient funds. The financial evidence should cover all applicants together, showing how every family member will be supported throughout the visa period.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">5</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Wait for approval or rejection together</h3>
            <p>If your student visa application is approved, your dependants' applications are typically approved as well (assuming all requirements are met). If the student visa is rejected, dependent applications may also be rejected. Approvals and refusals are usually issued together, not separately.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Common challenges ──────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Common Challenges With Family Applications</h2>
        <p>Being honest about potential difficulties helps you plan realistically. Here are the challenges we see most frequently:</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Consulate variation:</strong> Not all consulates handle family applications the same way. What is accepted in Madrid may be questioned in Barcelona. Some consulates are more restrictive about dependant applications on student visas than on other visa types. Clarify your consulate's specific policy early.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Financial threshold is significantly higher:</strong> Adding even one family member substantially increases the funds you must prove. On a student budget, this can be challenging. If you rely on family sponsorship, additional documentation is required, and consulates scrutinize sponsor finances carefully.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Multiple documents = exponential costs:</strong> Each family member needs apostille and sworn translation of every relevant document. A family of four could easily have 20–30+ documents requiring formal translation. Professional translation costs are substantial. Budget €2,000–4,000+ for a full family application.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Health insurance for multiple people is expensive:</strong> Family health insurance policies for visa purposes can cost €200–400+/month depending on ages. For a student on a tight budget, adding family members significantly increases total living costs.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Dependants typically cannot work:</strong> Your family members are likely not permitted to work on a dependant student visa. If your partner needs income, they may be better off applying for their own visa rather than as a dependant. This is a critical point to clarify with your consulate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn">
        <span class="msv__alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <p><strong>Longer processing times:</strong> Applications with multiple family members take longer to process. Expect timelines of 2–4 months or longer, especially if the consulate needs to verify financial documents or request additional information from multiple applicants.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Alternative options ──────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section--dark">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Alternative Options If Dependant Application Doesn't Work</h2>
        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If bringing your family as dependants on your student visa proves too complex, restrictive, or financially unfeasible, you have other options to consider:</p>
      </div>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.8rem;">
        <div>
          <h3 style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">Option 1: Your partner applies for their own visa</h3>
          <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); margin-bottom: 0.8rem;">Instead of being a dependant, your partner can apply independently. Options include:</p>
          <ul style="margin-left: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
            <li style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If they are self-employed or freelancing: <strong>Digital Nomad Visa</strong> (requires €2,300/month income and proof of client base)</li>
            <li style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If they have passive income: <strong>Non-Lucrative Visa</strong> (requires €1,260/month passive income in 2026, plus private health insurance)</li>
            <li style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If they want to work in Spain: Employment-based visa or work authorization (requires a Spanish employer and job offer)</li>
            <li style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If they are also studying: Their own <strong>Student Visa</strong></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">Option 2: Consider a different visa type for the whole family</h3>
          <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); margin-bottom: 0.8rem;">In some cases, if you and your family have other income sources or passive assets, a <strong><a href="/nlv-pillar-page.html" style="color: var(--yellow); font-weight: 700;">Non-Lucrative Visa</a></strong> might be more straightforward than a student visa with dependants. The NLV allows families to live together and has clearer rules for dependants. Check whether your household income would qualify.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">Option 3: Partner stays home until visa is approved</h3>
          <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">Some families choose to have the student move to Spain first, establish residency, and then the family member(s) join later once the student is settled. Once you have Spanish residency, bringing family members may be easier. This delays family reunification but can reduce immediate complexity.</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.9); margin-bottom: 0.6rem;">Option 4: Work with a specialist for dependant applications</h3>
          <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82);">If you are committed to bringing family as dependants, hiring a specialist immigration lawyer familiar with your specific consulate can dramatically improve your chances. They know local preferences, can navigate consulate variation, and can advise on the strongest way to present your case.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); margin-top: 2rem;"><a href="/dnv-pillar-page.html" style="color: var(--yellow); font-weight: 700;">Learn more about the Digital Nomad Visa</a> and <a href="/nlv-pillar-page.html" style="color: var(--yellow); font-weight: 700;">the Non-Lucrative Visa</a> to understand whether either might be a better fit for your family situation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

