  <!-- ── FAQs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Can my spouse or partner come to Spain on my student visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>In some circumstances, yes — your spouse or registered partner may apply as a dependant on your student visa. However, this is more complex than on other visa types and requires proving sufficient financial means to support them. Each consulate may have different rules, so it's essential to check with your specific consulate about their requirements and policies. Some consulates are more receptive to family applications than others.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can my children come to Spain on my student visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Dependent children under 18 may be able to apply as dependants on a student visa, provided you can demonstrate sufficient financial support for them. Older children or adult children can only apply as dependants in special circumstances where they can prove genuine financial dependence on you. Always check with your specific consulate about documentation and eligibility requirements for children.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much extra money do I need to show for dependants?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>As a general guide for 2026, you typically need to demonstrate an additional €450–600 per month (approximately 50% of IPREM) for each dependant, on top of your own student requirement. This means a student + spouse might need €1,350–1,500/month combined, and a student + two children might need €1,800–2,100/month. Always verify the exact amount with your specific consulate, as these thresholds are updated annually and may differ slightly by region.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does each family member need their own health insurance?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, absolutely. Every family member — including the main student and all dependants — needs their own private health insurance policy meeting Spanish visa requirements (no co-payment, full coverage in Spain, valid for the full duration, authorized insurer). You cannot combine multiple people under one policy. Budget €200–400+/month per person depending on age and health.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can my partner work in Spain on a dependant visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Typically, dependants on a student visa are not permitted to work. However, rules can vary by consulate and region. Some consulates may grant limited work rights under specific circumstances. This is a critical point to clarify directly with your consulate before applying — do not assume your partner can work. If your partner needs to work, they may be better served applying for their own visa (Digital Nomad, employment-based, etc.) rather than as a dependant.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if my partner wants their own visa instead of being a dependant?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>If your partner wants independent status, they can apply for their own visa. Options include the Digital Nomad Visa (if self-employed or freelancing with sufficient income), the Non-Lucrative Visa (if they have passive income), or employment-based visas. In some situations, this approach is actually simpler than navigating a dependant application on your student visa. We can help you evaluate which strategy works best for your family.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I bring my children to Spain on a student visa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Children under 18 can be included as dependants in a student visa application. Financial requirements increase for each dependant. Each child needs their own documentation (passport, birth certificate, photos). Children can attend Spanish state schools.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if I'm studying in Spain and my partner wants to join me?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>A spouse or civil partner can apply as a dependant on your student visa, provided you can demonstrate sufficient financial means for both of you. They would receive a dependant TIE card but may not automatically have the right to work — check current rules as this can change.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does a dependant's TIE card allow them to work in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Dependant family members on student visas do not automatically have work rights in Spain. They are resident as dependants, not as workers. Working requires obtaining a separate work authorisation. This is different from dependants on some other visa types.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do family members need health insurance too?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Each family member included in the application needs health insurance coverage, either on a family policy or individual policies. The same no-copayment, full Spain coverage requirement applies to each family member.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

